{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"annual-cover-renewal-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Review household insurance at renewal","description":"Takes a household through its yearly insurance renewals - list policies and dates, check what changed, re-quote like for like, compare and negotiate, then switch or renew without a gap in cover.","category":"insurance","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","review","plan"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["table","checklist","plan","message"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["financial"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["insurance-renewal","switching-insurer","renewal-premium","like-for-like","policy-calendar"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["review-insurance-coverage","check-insurance-after-life-change","compare-home-insurance-quotes","compare-car-insurance","choose-insurance-deductible","check-home-sum-insured"],"personas":["retired-claims-handler"]},"args":[{"name":"policies","description":"Each policy: type, renewal date, this year's and last year's premium, payment method, cover level, excess, add-ons, and the renewal notice if you have it. Leave out policy numbers.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"household","description":"What has changed in the last year (move, car, job, home working, building work, people moving in or out) and your country.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"calendar","file":"steps/01-calendar.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/01-calendar.md"},{"id":"changes","file":"steps/02-changes.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/02-changes.md"},{"id":"requote","file":"steps/03-requote.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/03-requote.md"},{"id":"compare","file":"steps/04-compare.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/04-compare.md"},{"id":"switch","file":"steps/05-switch.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"cover-renewal/05-switch.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs a household's insurance renewals the way a careful broker would for a client, without selling anything: put every policy and date on one calendar, update the facts insurers rely on, gather quotes on a genuinely like-for-like basis, compare on cover and price (including what the current insurer will offer to keep you), and renew or switch without leaving a day uncovered. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse approved facts.\n\n<policies>\n{{policies}}\n</policies>\n{{#household}}\n\n<household>\n{{household}}\n</household>\n{{/household}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only the figures and terms the person gives or confirms. Mark anything unknown and say where to find it (renewal notice, policy schedule, insurer account).\n- Do not recommend specific insurers or products and do not tell the person to cancel cover; describe options and trade-offs.\n- Compare cover before price. A cheaper quote that covers less is not a saving until the person decides the missing cover is not needed.\n- Never help misstate facts to get a lower price; accurate answers decide whether claims are paid.\n- Rules on renewal pricing, cooling-off periods and cancellation fees differ by country; present them as things to check.\n- Do not ask for policy numbers, logins or card details.","steps":[{"id":"calendar","file":"steps/01-calendar.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/01-calendar.md","text":"# Step 1: Policy calendar\n\n1. List every policy with renewal date, payment method (annual, monthly, auto-renew), this year's and last year's premium and the change, cover level and excess.\n2. Include cover held elsewhere: through bank accounts, cards, employers or memberships, and flag likely overlaps.\n3. Order by renewal date and mark which renewals fall in the next 6 weeks; note that quotes are often best sought a few weeks before renewal, not on the day.\n\nSections: Policy calendar, Cover held elsewhere, Overlaps to check, Unknowns. Stop for approval."},{"id":"changes","file":"steps/02-changes.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/02-changes.md","text":"# Step 2: What changed\n\n1. Go through changes in the last year for each policy: address, occupants, home working, lodgers or short lets, building work, valuables bought, vehicles, drivers, mileage, use, health or occupation for personal cover, claims made.\n2. For each change, say which policy it affects and whether the insurer must be told now rather than at renewal.\n3. Check sums insured and limits still fit (rebuild cost, contents, single items, travel destinations and activities).\n4. Note anything no longer needed (a sold car, a gadget policy on a phone that has been replaced).\n\nSections: Changes by policy, Tell the insurer now, Sums insured and limits to update, Cover possibly no longer needed. Stop for approval."},{"id":"requote","file":"steps/03-requote.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/03-requote.md","text":"# Step 3: Re-quote brief\n\n1. For each policy renewing soon, write a like-for-like quote brief: the confirmed facts, sums insured, excess, add-ons and the features that must not be dropped (for example accidental damage, away-from-home cover, protected no-claims discount, courtesy car, pre-existing medical conditions on travel cover).\n2. List where to get quotes (direct, comparison sites, a broker) without naming providers, and what to watch for on comparison sites (default excesses, stripped add-ons).\n3. Prepare a short script for the current insurer: ask for their best renewal price for the same cover, quoting the renewal notice and any lower like-for-like quote.\n\nSections: Quote briefs, Must-keep features, Where to quote, Script for the current insurer. Stop for approval while the person gathers quotes."},{"id":"compare","file":"steps/04-compare.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"cover-renewal/04-compare.md","text":"# Step 4: Compare\n\n1. Put the renewal offer and new quotes side by side per policy: price, sums insured, excesses (including special excesses), key limits, add-ons, and anything the quote does not mention.\n2. Work one or two realistic claims through each to show what each would pay.\n3. Separate quotes that are cheaper because they cover less from those that are cheaper for the same cover; for the former, state exactly what would be given up.\n4. Note the total yearly saving or extra cost across the household, and any cancellation or mid-term fees if switching.\n\nSections: Comparison by policy, Worked claims, Cheaper for less versus cheaper for the same, Household total, Decisions for you. Stop for the person's decision."},{"id":"switch","file":"steps/05-switch.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"cover-renewal/05-switch.md","text":"# Step 5: Renew or switch\n\n1. For each policy, a checklist in order: start the new policy on the day the old one ends (no gap), stop auto-renewal or cancel the old one in writing, cancel any direct debit only after cancellation is confirmed, and keep proof of no-claims or claims history the new insurer asks for.\n2. Note cooling-off periods and cancellation fees as things to check, and what to do if the old insurer renews automatically.\n3. Update any party that needs proof of cover (mortgage lender, landlord, vehicle registration or road authorities where required).\n4. Add next year's renewal dates and a reminder six weeks before each to the calendar, and file the documents.\n\nSections: Switch checklist by policy, Things to check, Who to tell, Next year's calendar."}]}
